orians he's part of the sort of  introductory 20th century survey and he  writes this article he publishes it in  the United States and in it he  as he sort of posits this hypothetical  comparison between two painters one of  them is Pablo Picasso and the other is  as it turns out ilya repin this is maybe  the only reference to ilya repin in all  English language scholarship of this  time what's really remarkable and what  he says is imagine a Russian peasant  looking at an abstract painting by ilya  repin or excuse me by pablo picasso and  a realist painting by ilya repin this  peasant will look at the cost oh and not  understand it right it will be foreign  it will be unusual there will be no sort  of connection his own reality but we'll  look at the repin and say yes I get it  this relates to me and he uses this to  say Picasso is avant-garde art and repin  is kitsch hi is predigested is far too  easy so this is a moment where the  realist tradition is really rejected for  Western modernist and this is why it  doesn't make it into the Canon in a  later edition of this article Greenberg  will in fact include an addendum and say  actually at the time I realized much  later that at the time I had confused  reckon with somebody else and I'd never  actually seen any of his work my  goodness and so my sort of project or my  method then my method of research was to  kind of correct what Greenberg does  wrong and to actually look at these  these paintings and so I look at them  and I spent a lot of time with each one  of them because I wanted to make an  argument that they were in fact worthy  of extended analysis and worthy of  looking at very carefully and for long  stretches of time okay do you think that  the artists and writers of that period  conversed in any way or have any kind of  conversations were they known to each  other for instance definitely the  Russian artistic sphere at this point  artistic world is very small and mostly  centered in Moscow and st. Petersburg  they're quite simply we're not that many  painters and writers and so they all  knew one another they were all  communicating with one another they did  occupy somewhat different social spheres  which  actually becomes an important part of  the argument writers tended to be of a  higher social class and painters we can  interest we can think for example of  count Lev Tolstoy versus somebody like  idiot repin who is born in a very modest  military settlement in Ukraine so this  also becomes part of the story they  interact but they're not exactly of the  same circle and they're always sort of  lobbying for attention and in particular  the painters are always sort of trying  to become just as good as the literary  tradition so there's quite a bit of  competition between the two okay years  now when you look at the paintings and  read the book is there any correlation  that you can find between you know  painting showing up and written work or  you know kind of written work being  illustrated somehow in paintings  mm-hmm there's quite a bit of crossover  you'll get really explicit examples so  Tolstoy and Anna Karenina will write  about an artist - a Russian artist who's  living in Rome and he in fact paints the  portrait of Honor herself in the save  Sookie's novel the idiot we get explicit  references to whole lines painting of a  painting of dead Christ laying in his  tomb becomes a kind of emblem for the  novel as a whole so there are moments  like this and I do speak about those  although I'm also interested in modes of  representation such as what such as for  example when you're reading a novel and  you come across across a very lengthy  and very picturesque description of a  landscape for me this is a moment when  the novel is sort of turning toward a  visual artistic medium and thinking  about this other art form rather  explicitly and both what it wants to  borrow from it but also how it wants to  distinguish itself from it you'll see  this in painting too with narrative  painting where the painting itself is  sort of trying to tell a story but also  in some sense be optically interesting  okay so who are some of the writers and  artists that you feature in your book  and why mm-hmm  so I hit all  classic as I've mentioned already to  Gainey of Tolstoy Dostoyevsky and for  the painters repin but I also do talk  about some of the lesser-known artists a  good example of this would be a group of  writers and artists who are practicing  in st. Petersburg in the 1840s who call  themselves the natural school and they  are a very small circle again mostly  students the stay of skin toward any of  our part of the circle at one point but  they really their work is marked by this  desire to walk through the city much  like a student would you live in these  not the greatest neighborhood you spend  a lot of your time outdoors walking  through the city sort of conversing with  your friends at cafes and on sidewalks  and they build an artistic aesthetic  based on these experiences of urban life  one of my painters for example pavia  Botha from this period will actually  walk through the cities and peek through  people's windows and he's trying to fix  details from different windows a  chandelier here a scene he witnessed  here a wallpaper design here and he puts  them into these small genre scenes of  merchant life okay and how do you know  that was his technique he writes about  it and in fact his right his friends  will write about it too he was known for  this but he would get up every morning  he would go outside he would walk around  and st. Petersburg in a particular area  is known for having street-level  apartments so it will have windows that  line the street level and he will  discuss the very process of standing and  looking inside of a window and so we see  this again and again in the memoirs and  one of the things I'm arguing that in  that particular chapter is that we as  viewers looking at these paintings whi