r submission this was  the topic of the painting repens just  beginning to work on it so he shows  Tolstoy to studies and Tolstoy says  they're not very good there's really no  main idea here Wow and weapons really  disturbed this old boy telling you this  and he thinks about it for a few days  and he finally writes Tolstoy back and  he says I appreciate your advice but I'm  going to keep going and I think the main  point is here that perhaps idea means  something very different for a writer  than it does for a painter and it's this  moment where repin realizes that in fact  an idea or even realism itself  might mean something very different in  literature than in painting and that  it's important for him to in some sense  answer this call to realism in a way  that respects his own medium and so this  is one of the big sort of questions that  I'm dealing with in the book is how  these two different art forms really  sort of answer the call of realism in in  very specific particular ways okay and  in doing your research did you look at  what is going on in the rest of the  world during this time period were these  Russian artists influenced by you know  things that are going on in France for  instance or the United States or was it  pretty much an isolated kind of  environment I mean they are very much in  dialogue with what's going on elsewhere  again realism is a transnational  movement it is going on everywhere and  you know one of the interesting aspects  of Russia's particular engagement with  this is that they do it a little bit  later than the rest of the world this is  is almost a cliche of Russian culture  they come to everything a little bit  late and so one of the questions I do  ask them is sort of how much are they  modeling themselves after Western Europe  and how much are they charting their own  path it's a bit of a mixed bag actually  the Russians definitely are in dialogue  they have some connections with what's  going on in France mostly but they  