really do attempt to do their own thing  again as I mentioned with Dostoyevsky  he's aiming for the sort of spiritual  realism right this is a kind of realism  that's very different than Balzac who  has a sociological one just as key  legitimately believes that one could in  fact inspire almost Transfiguration in a  novel and so it has a sort of magical  transcendent quality that you would  never encounter anywhere else in the  painterly tradition one argument that is  frequently made is that Russian painting  of this period is more interested in  political narrative okay this is not  entirely the case okay I was going to  ask you are there any kind of political  or government restrictions or issues  with artists and what they write  and paints there are I mean you know  censorship laws are still very much in  place there are a few sort of dramatic  stories of paintings and works of  literature that our either's are taken  down from exhibitions are not allowed to  be published so this is an issue part of  the question with interpreting these  works is that again the Soviet influence  is so strong  the Soviets really wanted to read all of  this as a kind of proto Soviet history  so for them all of these paintings of  everyday life were also about you know  the downtrodden peasants and you know  these were all Pro cursors - the  precursors to the Socialists in the 20th  century and they didn't want to think  about aesthetic issues because for them  technique style form what the painting  looked like would have been a moment of  kind of bourgeois formalism and so part  of what I'm trying to do is recuperate  both sides of these argument say well  actually all of these artists Tolstoy  repin all of them are in some sense  equally interested both in political  ideology and in sort of big spiritual  messages and in technique and style in  something that I think we're more  comfortable thinking of when we consider  French realism or American realism okay  also what do you conclude in your books  so I would say I have two main takeaways  okay or at least this is what I hope now  beyond just you know all of the shore  interesting story yeah the first one  would be that the classic Russian novel  does not emerge in a vacuum that it  emerges very much in dialogue with other  art forms I talk about painting but of  course music and the Performing Arts are  there as well and that it really emerges  as part of this vibrant dynamic cultural  universe and the other one is that  Russian painting of this period is  world-class and it is worthy of being  analyzed as being considered being  looked at that it stands and should  stand next to these celebrated novels of  Tolstoy Dostoevsky but that it should  also stand next to the realist  masterpiece  of Cour de of Akins of Menzel of western  painters were much more familiar with  okay this has been fascinating thank you  thank you very much okay for more  information about Professor Bronson and  her research please visit our website at  macmillan report yale.edu be sure to  join us again for another episode of the  Macmillan report made possible through  funding from the Whitney and Eddie  Macmillan Centre for International and  area studies at Yale  [Music]
 

